= Garlic

_Status: fully classical in all four canonical Eurasian medical traditions, and one of the_ tri- or tetra-system convergence drugs _of the materia medica — Ayurveda (laśuna, the famous_ six-rasa drug _by Bhāvaprakāśa attribution), Classical Chinese Medicine (大蒜 dàsuàn, in the Bencao tradition as an imported drug from the Western Regions), Tibb (ثوم_ thūm_, Avicennan_ Hot-and-Dry in the 4th degree _and the canonical_ Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ _"poor man's theriac"), and Greco-Roman medicine (σκόροδον / allium, with Hippocratic, Dioscoridean, Plinian, and Galenic substantive treatment). Modern Western pharmacology has rehabilitated garlic via the_ allicin / diallyl-sulfide _chemistry as the canonical antimicrobial-and-cardiovascular herb. The classical etymological-and-mythological registers are unusually rich: Ayurveda has the_ Rahu-soma origin myth _(garlic as the drops of immortality-soma that fell to earth from the demon Rahu's severed head); Egypt has the_ pyramid-workers garlic-and-onion ration _documented by Herodotus; Rome has the_ moretum garlic-paste of Virgil _and the gladiator-and-soldier garlic ration; Islam has the_ Tibb al-Nabawī _careful boundary between medicinal use (permitted) and mosque-attendance (prohibited, because of the breath)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Allium sativum_ L., family Amaryllidaceae (formerly Alliaceae, formerly Liliaceae — the species relationships have been resorted under APG phylogeny; modern placement is Amaryllidaceae subfamily Allioideae). The drug-foodstuff is the *bulb*, which consists of multiple *cloves* (the individual bulblets). The pharmacy term is _Allii sativi bulbus_.

_Allium_ is the classical Latin name; the further etymology is uncertain (possibly from a pre-Latin Mediterranean substrate), with proposed connections to Greek ἀλέω (_aléō_, "to grind") or to Indo-European roots for "smelly / pungent." _sativum_ = "cultivated, sown." Related Allium species treated in medicine:
- *_Allium cepa_ L.* — onion (Latin _cepa_; Greek _krommyon_).
- *_Allium ampeloprasum_ L.* — leek (Latin _porrum_) and *elephant garlic* variety.
- *_Allium schoenoprasum_ L.* — chive.
- *_Allium ursinum_ L.* — *ramsons / wild garlic / bear's garlic*; the European-wild form, foundational to Germanic / Slavic folk medicine.
- *_Allium tuberosum_ Rottler ex Spreng.* — *Chinese chive (韭菜 jiǔcài)*; the canonical Chinese leafy Allium.
- *_Allium fistulosum_ L.* — *Welsh onion / scallion / 蔥 cōng* in Chinese cuisine.
- *_Allium chinense_ G. Don* — *Chinese onion / 薤 xiè / 蕗蕎 lùqiáo*.

The *Allium genus* in classical pharmacy carries the broad "pungent-warming-antimicrobial" register across all its species; this entry focuses on _A. sativum_ specifically.#footnote[Etymology and species: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _allium_, _cepa_, _porrum_); the further etymology of _allium_ is uncertain, with the Mediterranean-substrate hypothesis the most accepted modern position. Species placement: APG IV phylogeny (2016).]

*English:* _Garlic_, from Old English _gārlēac_ — *_gār_* (spear / lance / sharp-pointed object) + _lēac_ (leek / generic Allium). "Spear-leek," referring to the spear-shaped leaves or to the pungent character. The Germanic-language family preserves variants: German _Knoblauch_ (from Old High German _klobloh_, "split-leek," referring to the clove structure), Dutch _knoflook_, Swedish _vitlök_ ("white-leek"). The English word preserves the Anglo-Saxon naming logic distinct from the Latin-Greek tradition.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "garlic, n."); the Old English _gār_-_lēac_ etymology is standard. The Germanic-vs-Romance etymological split for garlic is one of the cleaner Indo-European naming divergences.]

*Sanskrit:* लशुन (_laśuna_) — the canonical classical name; also लसुन (_lasuna_, the more common modern Sanskrit spelling), in modern Hindi लहसुन (_lahsun_). The classical Sanskrit nighaṇṭu tradition gives substantial synonym lists: रसोन (_rasona_, "one taste less" — referring to the famous classical attribution that garlic possesses five of six rasas and lacks only one; see Ayurvedic section), महौषध (_mahauṣadha_, "the great medicine" — the highest classical praise, applied also to dried ginger), उग्रगन्ध (_ugragandha_, "strong-smelling"), अरिष्ट (_ariṣṭa_, "auspicious / never-failing"), भूतघ्न (_bhūtaghna_, "ghost-destroying" — the classical-apotropaic register).#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _laśuna_, _rasona_, _mahauṣadha_, _ugragandha_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_, _laśuna_ entry — Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba. The _rasona_ "one-taste-less" etymology is universally cited in classical commentary and is the basis of the famous six-rasa / lacks-one-rasa Ayurvedic attribution.]

*The Rahu-soma origin myth:* a foundational Sanskrit-Purāṇic narrative explaining the simultaneous divine-medicinal-and-tamasic / demonic character of garlic. The story: during the *churning of the ocean* (_samudra-manthana_) by the devas and asuras to obtain the soma / amṛta (nectar of immortality), the demon *Rahu* disguised himself as a deva and drank from the soma; *Viṣṇu* (sometimes Mohinī) cut off his head, but the soma had already reached his throat — so the severed head became immortal as the lunar-eclipse-causing demon Rahu. *Drops of soma fell from Rahu's severed throat to the earth and grew into garlic*. The myth explains: (a) why garlic has divine medicinal potency (it is literally drops of immortality nectar); (b) why garlic is *forbidden* in orthodox brahminical-and-temple ritual contexts (it grew from a demon's throat); (c) why garlic is paradoxically prescribed as a rasāyana in Caraka and the Ayurvedic medical tradition while excluded from sāttvic vegetarian dietetics (the dual register).#footnote[Rahu-soma origin myth: Purāṇic literature (notably Bhāgavata Purāṇa, Viṣṇu Purāṇa); standard Hindu-mythology literature (e.g. Doniger, _Hindu Myths_). The myth's role in justifying the dual sacred-medicinal-and-tamasic character of garlic is universal in classical Ayurvedic commentary.]

*Chinese:* *大蒜 (dàsuàn, "big garlic")* — the canonical Chinese name; *蒜 (suàn)* alone for the general genus. Historical note: the character 蒜 and the substance are documented in Chinese literature from the Han period; garlic is recorded as having been introduced to China from the *Western Regions* (西域, Central Asia and Persia) during the Han embassy of *Zhang Qian (張騫)* in the late 2nd c. BCE — making garlic one of the foundational "exotic vegetables" (胡菜 _húcài_, "barbarian vegetables") of Han-period agricultural introduction, alongside cucumber, alfalfa, sesame, and pepper. Sub-classifications:
- *獨蒜 / 獨頭蒜 (dú suàn / dú tóu suàn, "single-headed garlic")* — the single-clove cultivar (Chinese-specific cultivar; treated as a separate drug in some classical texts).
- *青蒜 (qīng suàn, "green garlic")* — fresh young garlic stalks.
- *蒜苗 / 蒜薹 (suànmiáo / suàntái)* — garlic scapes / shoots.
- *黑蒜 (hēi suàn, "black garlic")* — *modern* (20th-c. Korean/Japanese invention) — aged-fermented garlic at controlled temperature and humidity, sweetening dramatically and transforming the allicin chemistry. Post-classical but increasingly prominent in modern Asian and Western functional-food use.#footnote[Han-period introduction: Zhang Qian's embassy to the Western Regions is recorded in _Shi Ji_ (史記) and _Han Shu_ (漢書); the "barbarian vegetables" register is preserved in many Chinese plant-names with the 胡 prefix. The single-clove cultivar (_dú tóu suàn_) is treated as distinctively warming in Chinese folk dietetics. Black garlic (_hēi suàn_): modern Korean / Japanese invention, post-classical, but with substantial 21st-c. global functional-food market.]

*Arabic:* *ثوم (_thūm_)* — the canonical Arabic name; the same word in Persian and (with vocalization) in Urdu/Hakeemi (although _lasan / lahsun_ is the more common Hindi-Urdu term). The medieval Arabic literature also uses *فوم (_fūm_)* — found in the Qur'ān 2:61 (the only Qur'ānic occurrence of garlic, in the verse where the Israelites in the wilderness complain of manna and quail and demand the *fūm* and onions and lentils of Egypt — the standard exegetical identification of *fūm* is garlic, though some commentators interpret it as wheat). *Persian:* سیر (_sīr_); *Urdu / Hakeemi:* لہسن (_lahsun_) and ثوم.

*Greek:* σκόροδον (_skórodon_); the Hippocratic and Dioscoridean foundational term. The further Indo-European etymology is uncertain.

*Tibb al-Nabawī register*: the Prophet's recommendation against eating garlic before attending the mosque (because of the breath odor — out of consideration for the angels and other worshippers) is one of the most-cited Prophetic-medicine references. The hadith does *not* prohibit garlic itself — Islamic jurisprudence consistently affirms its medicinal permissibility — but specifies the *contextual etiquette*: eat garlic; cook it well; do not attend the mosque immediately after raw-garlic consumption (Bukhari, _Kitāb al-Adhān_; Muslim).#footnote[Tibb al-Nabawī garlic: Qur'ān 2:61 (the *fūm* verse, in Sūrat al-Baqarah, with standard exegetical identification as garlic); Bukhari, _Kitāb al-Adhān_, and Muslim, _Kitāb al-Masājid_, for the mosque-breath hadith. Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya treats garlic at length in _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

大蒜 is canonical in the Chinese Bencao tradition from the Han period onward; the principal entry is in the _Bencao Gangmu_ (Li Shizhen, 1578) under the "vegetable" section. The drug is treated as warming-pungent with substantial antimicrobial and parasiticidal use.#footnote[大蒜 entry: _Bencao Gangmu_, 1578; documented in earlier sources including _Mingyi Bielu_ (名醫別錄). Systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004).]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫); some sources say hot (熱) for the dú tóu suàn / single-clove cultivar.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Pungent (辛).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺); some sources add Large Intestine (大腸).

*Actions & indications:*
- 解毒消腫 — *resolves toxicity and disperses swelling* — the cardinal action; for furuncles, carbuncles, snake-and-insect bite, scrofula. Topical garlic preparations for skin-and-soft-tissue infections.
- 殺蟲 — *anthelminthic* — kills worms (the canonical Chinese pharmacy parasiticidal indication); for pinworm, hookworm, roundworm.
- 健脾消滯 — strengthens Spleen and disperses food stagnation; for cold-pattern dyspepsia, retention of food.
- 溫中行氣 — warms the middle and moves qi (for cold-pattern abdominal pain).
- 止痢 — stops dysentery (the famous classical use, for damp-heat or damp-cold dysentery — the modern correlate is the antimicrobial activity).
- 預防瘟疫 — *prevents pestilence* (the classical "carry-a-clove" prophylactic during epidemics — paralleling the European plague-era garlic-wearing tradition).
- *Topical: snake-and-insect-bite; ringworm; tinea pedis ("athlete's foot")* — the classical antimicrobial-topical use; modern allicin chemistry vindicates this directly.

*Cautions:* yin-vacuity with heat patterns; pregnancy at high therapeutic dose; eye-and-throat irritation from raw use; topical use can cause *contact bullous dermatitis* (real and documented in modern dermatology literature — classical pharmacy was aware of this).#footnote[Action list: _Bencao Gangmu_ 大蒜 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The plague-prophylactic and topical-antimicrobial uses converge across systems and are well-vindicated by modern allicin chemistry.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Laśuna is *fully classical in Ayurveda*, with substantive treatment in Caraka, Suśruta, and Bhāvaprakāśa. The famous classical attribution is the *five-rasa / six-minus-one* taste classification (also called _rasona_, "one-taste-less"), with various sources differing on which rasa is absent.#footnote[Laśuna in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (annapānavidhi, vegetable section); Cikitsāsthāna 1.4 (rasāyana-pāda — the "Rahu-soma" origin and the laśuna rasāyana indication); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_, _laśuna_ entry. Translations: Sharma & Dash (Chowkhamba); Murthy (Chaukhamba).]

*Rasa (taste):* The famous classical attribution is that laśuna possesses *five of six rasas* — the standard Bhāvaprakāśa attribution gives *pungent (kaṭu) in the root, sweet (madhura) in the leaves, salty (lavaṇa) in the stem, astringent (kaṣāya) in the top, bitter (tikta) in the seed*, with the sixth rasa (*sour, amla*) absent. Other recensions distribute the rasas differently, but the *five-rasa attribution* is universal. The *rasona* etymology of the synonym "one-taste-less" preserves this classical reading.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (heavy), Snigdha (unctuous — unusual for a pungent drug; reflects the high garlic-oil content), Tīkṣṇa (sharp), Picchila (slimy), Sara (mobile / spreading).

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (warming) — strongly so.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (pungent).#footnote[Rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka of laśuna: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; the unusual guru-snigdha-tīkṣṇa-sara guṇa profile is the basis of laśuna's distinctive _vāta-pacifying-while-tīkṣṇa_ character. The five-rasa attribution is in Bhāvaprakāśa and earlier nighaṇṭus.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* The classical commentary highlights laśuna's *combined Vāta-pacifying-and-Kapha-clearing-while-pungent* action — typical pungent-warming drugs aggravate Vāta (the rūkṣa-laghu-tīkṣṇa being Vāta-aggravating), but laśuna is uniquely *Vāta-pacifying* via the snigdha-picchila-guru-sara guṇa modifier. This *Vāta-pacifying-pungent* combination is read at prabhāva level in classical commentary, and underwrites laśuna's signature use as a *rasāyana for Vāta-vyādhi* (degenerative-neurological-musculoskeletal Vāta disorders). Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1.4 gives a substantial laśuna rasāyana protocol.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Vāta and Kapha* (the snigdha-tīkṣṇa-uṣṇa profile against Vāta; the laghu-tīkṣṇa-kaṭu-vipāka against Kapha); *aggravates Pitta* (strongly, due to the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa). The classical reading: laśuna is the *premier Vāta-Kapha drug* with strong Pitta caution.

*Karma (actions):*
- _Rasāyana_ — rejuvenative; specifically a *Vāta-vyādhi rasāyana* (Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1.4).
- _Vājīkara_, _vṛṣya_ — virilifying, aphrodisiac.
- _Balya_, _bṛṃhaṇa_ — strengthening, nourishing.
- _Medhya_ — intellect-promoting (a perhaps-surprising attribution given the tamasic register; classical commentary defends it on the basis of the medicinal-vs-dietary distinction).
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — kindles digestion, supports āma-digestion.
- _Krimi-ghna_ — anthelminthic (the cross-system convergence with Chinese 殺蟲 and Greek-Roman uses).
- _Kāsa-hara, śvāsa-hara_ — relieves cough and dyspnea (cold-pattern and chronic).
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic; modern cardiovascular evidence supports this.
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta stagnation; useful in chronic constipation, gas, bloating.
- _Indriya-balya_ — strengthens the senses (especially hearing — the classical _karṇa-rogāhara_ indication for ear disorders).
- _Vraṇa-ropaṇa_ — wound-healing (topical and internal).
- _Viṣa-ghna_ — anti-toxin (snakebite, scorpion-sting, insect-bite).
- _Apasmāra-hara_ — anti-epileptic; classical use in convulsive disorders.
- _Bhūta-vidyā register_ (the "ghost-medicine" category): laśuna as an apotropaic substance against possession and malign-spirit affliction — the classical _bhūtaghna_ attribution.

Used in: _vātavyādhi_ (the principal indication — chronic joint and neurological Vāta disorders); _kāsa, śvāsa_ (chronic cough, asthma); _krimi-roga_ (intestinal parasites); _hṛd-roga_ (cardiac disease, modern correlate); _arśas_ (hemorrhoids); _gulma_ (abdominal masses); _ślīpada_ (filariasis); _karṇa-roga_ (ear disorders); _viṣa_ (poisoning); _apasmāra_; _kuṣṭha_ (skin disorders, topical); _kuṣṭha-ghna_ in compound.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 and Cikitsāsthāna 1.4 (rasāyana), 28 (vātavyādhi); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; standard Ayurvedic clinical practice. The Vāta-vyādhi rasāyana use is the foundational classical indication.]

*The classical laśuna-kṣīrapāka rasāyana protocol*: garlic cooked in milk (1 part garlic juice to 8 parts milk, reduced by simmering) and taken as a *cold-season rasāyana* over 1-3 months for chronic Vāta-vyādhi, joint disease, and convalescent recovery. The milk-cooking saṃskāra is constitutive — it removes the harsh-pungent-irritant edge of raw garlic while preserving the warming-tonifying register and adding the nourishing milk fraction. The protocol parallels the famous "*lasooney ka doodh*" (garlic milk) of modern North-Indian folk medicine. Caraka specifies the season (winter, _hemanta_-_śiśira_), the dose, and the dietary restrictions.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(S/N) Conditional cautions:*
  - *Pitta excess*, *amlapitta* (hyperacidity), *raktapitta* (bleeding-from-heat), high fever — the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa profile inflames Pitta; laśuna is *strongly* Pitta-aggravating.
  - *Summer use* — the season caution; restrict to winter rasāyana use classically.
  - *Pregnancy at high therapeutic dose* — the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa register; small culinary use is accepted in dietetic tradition.
- *(S) Saṃyoga viruddha:* heavy combinations with sour-fermented foods aggravate Pitta further; with strong heating spices in excess.
- *(F) Folk / religious:* *the broader sāttvic-dietetic exclusion* — orthodox brahminical, temple-priestly, and yogic dietary traditions classify laśuna (with onion) as *tamasic* (inducing inertia / dullness / sensual coarseness) and exclude it from spiritual-practice diet. The classification rests on the Rahu-soma origin myth and on the assessment that the substance's strong tīkṣṇa-uṣṇa-prabhāva action disturbs mental subtlety. *This is a religious-dietary exclusion, not a medical contraindication* — Ayurvedic medical tradition consistently uses laśuna as a medicine even where the same tradition excludes it as a daily food. The dual register is one of the cleaner classical examples of medical-religious dietary differentiation.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Anticoagulant interaction*: allicin and related sulfides have substantial antiplatelet activity; *high-dose garlic + warfarin / NOAC / aspirin / clopidogrel* increases bleeding risk; pre-surgical garlic restriction is standard pharmacy advice (avoid high-dose garlic 7-10 days before surgery).
  - *Hypoglycemic / antidiabetic interaction*: garlic has modest hypoglycemic effect; theoretical additive with antidiabetic drugs.
  - *HIV protease inhibitor interaction* (saquinavir specifically) — garlic reduces saquinavir plasma concentration; documented and clinically significant.
  - *Pregnancy*: dietary use accepted; high-dose supplements not recommended.
  - *GI irritation*: raw garlic on empty stomach causes substantial irritation; the classical milk-cooking saṃskāra addresses this.
  - *Topical contact bullous dermatitis*: undiluted raw garlic on skin (especially prolonged contact) causes *chemical burns* with bullous dermatitis — documented in modern dermatology literature. The classical preparation of macerated garlic plasters typically diluted with oil or honey precisely to avoid this.
  - *Halitosis-and-body-odor*: documented; the sulfur metabolites (allyl methyl sulfide) are excreted through skin, lungs, and urine for ~24-48 hours after consumption.
  - *Botulism* (rare): improperly-preserved raw-garlic-in-oil preparations have been the source of botulism outbreaks; use commercial garlic-in-oil with acid preservation, or refrigerate home preparations and use within days.#footnote[Anticoagulant interaction, hypoglycemic interaction, HIV protease inhibitor interaction: documented in herb-drug-interaction literature (Mills & Bone, _Herb Contraindications and Drug Interactions_); recent reviews in _Phytomedicine_ and _Drug Safety_. Topical bullous dermatitis: documented in dermatology case reports; the classical macerated-garlic-plaster traditions invariably diluted with oil or honey, precisely to avoid this. Garlic-in-oil botulism: documented FDA-CDC outbreak literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Garlic is among the most-praised drugs of the entire Tibb pharmacopoeia, with Avicennan substantive treatment, the canonical_ Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ ("poor man's theriac") _appellation, and Qur'ānic+hadith attestation. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* ثوم (_thūm_) — the canonical name; *فوم (_fūm_)* in Qur'ān 2:61 (the standard exegetical identification); ثوم بستاني (_thūm bustānī_, "garden garlic") for the cultivated form; ثوم بري (_thūm barrī_, "wild garlic") for _A. ursinum_ and related. Persian: سیر (_sīr_); سیر باغی (_sīr-e bāghī_). Urdu/Hakeemi: لہسن (_lahsun_); ثوم (_thūm_) in formal Unani register.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *Qur'ān 2:61* (Sūrat al-Baqarah) — the verse where the Israelites in the Sinai wilderness complain of manna and quail and demand the *fūm* (standardly identified as garlic), onions, lentils, cucumbers, and herbs of Egypt. The verse establishes garlic in the Qur'ānic dietary register as a *legitimate but lower-grade food* compared to the manna-and-quail. Hadith: *Bukhari, Kitāb al-Adhān; Muslim, Kitāb al-Masājid* — *the mosque-breath rule*: the Prophet recommended that whoever has eaten garlic, onion, or leek should not come to the mosque until the smell has dissipated, *out of consideration for the angels and other worshippers*. This is *not a prohibition of garlic* — Islamic jurisprudence consistently affirms its medicinal permissibility — but specifies the contextual etiquette. Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya treats garlic at length in *al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī*, calling it among the most-praised medicinal foods.

*Mizāj (temperament):* *Hot and Dry in the 4th degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb*, Book II, _thūm_; corroborated by Ibn al-Bayṭār and Anṭākī). The *4th-degree classification is exceptional* — only a few substances in the Tibb pharmacopoeia reach the 4th degree, and these are by definition *potent-and-dangerous-and-requiring-strict-dose-control*. The 4th-degree placement converges with the Ayurvedic *strongly-uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa* reading and with the Chinese *warm-to-hot* classification — tri-system convergence on the strong-warming-pungent character.

*Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ (ترياق الفقراء, "the poor man's theriac")* — the canonical Tibb appellation for garlic. *Theriac (تِرْياق _tiryāq_)* was the foundational Greco-Arabic-medieval-European *universal antidote* — a complex compound preparation (the most famous being *Theriac Andromachi*, the imperial Roman theriac of ~60 ingredients including viper-flesh) used as protection against poison, plague, and the broad humoral disorders. The treatment was extraordinarily expensive and was reserved for the wealthy. *Garlic was called "the poor man's theriac" because it was held to provide the same antidote-and-prophylactic protection at vanishing cost.* The classical attribution is universal across Arabic Tibb, medieval European pharmacy, and Renaissance herbalism. The modern correlate: garlic's broad-spectrum antimicrobial activity does in fact provide a substantive antimicrobial protective register — the classical "antidote" framing has empirical basis.#footnote[Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _thūm_ entry; the appellation is universal in Arabic Tibb literature. Theriac history: standard pharmacy history (e.g. Watson, _Theriac and Mithridatium: A Study in Therapeutics_); the most famous theriac (Theriac Andromachi) was the imperial Roman preparation. The "poor man's theriac" framing of garlic is one of the cleaner cross-system classical-modern alignments — the modern allicin antimicrobial chemistry vindicates the classical antidote register substantively.]

*Af'āl (actions):*
- *Muqawwī al-jasad* (body-strengthening); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī* (vital-spirit tonic); *muqawwī al-maʿida* (gastric tonic).
- *Muḥallil li-l-suddad* (resolves obstructions); *mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* (deobstruent).
- *Mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic); *mufattit li-l-ḥaṣā* (stone-breaking — paralleling tribulus and the Ayurvedic aśmari-bhedana).
- *Qātil li-l-dūd* (anthelminthic — the canonical Tibb parasiticidal indication, converging with Chinese 殺蟲 and Greek tradition).
- *Munaffith* (expectorant); for chronic phlegmatic cough.
- *Muḥarrik al-bāh* (aphrodisiac).
- *Muqāwim li-l-sumūm* (anti-toxin — the Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ register).
- *Mughayyir li-l-balgham al-yābis* (modifies dry phlegm).
- *Muqaṭṭiʿ* (cuts viscous humors).
- *Topical: muḥallil li-l-aurām* (resolves swellings); for furuncles, contusions, scorpion-and-snakebite.

*Istemālāt (indications):* phlegmatic diseases broadly (the principal humoral target); chronic cough; chronic asthma; intestinal parasites; cold-pattern dyspepsia; renal and bladder stones; impotence (mild); chronic abdominal pain of cold pattern; poisoning (especially scorpion and snake — internal and topical); cold-pattern paralysis; epilepsy; chronic skin disorders (topical).

*Mawāne' (contraindications):* bilious / hot-pattern conditions (*amrāḍ al-ṣafrā'*); the 4th-degree Hot-Dry classification makes garlic *strongly contraindicated* in bilious excess; pregnancy at therapeutic dose; severe peptic ulcer; severe heat-pattern eye disease; nursing mothers in heavy use (transmission to breast milk; affects infant flavor preferences). The *mosque-attendance* etiquette caution (from Tibb al-Nabawī) is a religious-jurisprudential point, not a medical contraindication.

*Muṣliḥ (corrective adjuvant):* *vinegar (khall)* — moderates the over-heating; *mint (naʿnaʿ)* — moderates the breath; *almond oil (duhn al-lawz)* and *coriander seed (kuzbara)* for the over-irritating tendency; *honey (ʿasal)* in the famous *thūm-and-honey* preparation. The Persian-Levantine *thūmiyya* (garlic-and-oil-and-vinegar emulsion sauce, the ancestor of modern aïoli and *toum*) observes the muṣliḥ rule precisely.

*Badal (substitute):* onion (*baṣal*); shallot (*basal akhḍar*) for milder action; horseradish (*fijl ḥārr*) for the strongly-pungent pharmacological function in northern European contexts.

*Tibb formulas:*
- *Sufūf-i Thūm* — garlic powder; for chronic phlegmatic conditions.
- *Joshānda-yi Thūm wa-Maʿz* (garlic-and-fennel decoction) — digestive.
- *Marham-i Thūm* — garlic ointment; topical for swellings and skin disorders.
- *Toum (تُوم)* — the Levantine garlic-emulsion sauce (garlic + lemon + olive oil + salt, whipped to a stiff emulsion); culinary-medical at the boundary; the Mediterranean *aïoli* (Provençal) and *alioli* (Catalan) descend from this preparation.
- *Sikanjabīn-i Thūm* — garlic vinegar honey-syrup; the *Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ* preparation in syrup form.
- *Ḥabb-i Mūsh* and other compound aphrodisiac maʿjūns include garlic.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _thūm_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*, _thūm_ entry — comprehensive Andalusian-Maghrebi treatment; al-Rāzī, *al-Ḥāwī*; Anṭākī, *Tadhkirah*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _lahsun / thūm_ entry — modern Urdu Unani standard; Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, *al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī*, _thūm_ chapter.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *大蒜散 / 大蒜湯* — single-herb garlic powder / decoction; for cold-pattern dysentery, anthelminthic use, common cold prophylaxis. Typical dose: 3-9 g cooked / decocted; smaller for raw.
- *獨蒜灸 / 隔蒜灸 (dú suàn jiǔ / gé suàn jiǔ, "single-clove moxibustion" / "moxa-on-garlic")* — the classical *moxibustion-through-garlic* technique: a slice of single-clove garlic placed on the skin and moxa cone burned on top; for chronic abscesses, scrofula, and certain Vāta-equivalent stagnation patterns. Widely used in modern TCM acupuncture practice.
- *蒜泥 (suàn ní)* — garlic paste; topical for snake-and-insect-bite and infected wounds.
- *糖醋蒜 / 醋蒜 (táng cù suàn / cù suàn)* — vinegar-pickled garlic; the canonical Chinese functional-food preparation, eaten as a digestive condiment.
- *青蒜 / 蒜苗* — fresh green garlic stalks; dietary register.
- *黑蒜 (hēi suàn)* — *modern* (20th-c. Korean/Japanese invention) aged-fermented black garlic; sweet, melodious, with substantially reduced allicin pungency; modern functional-food and culinary use.

=== Ayurveda

- *Laśuna kṣīrapāka (लशुनक्षीरपाक)* — garlic-cooked-in-milk; the canonical Caraka rasāyana preparation (Cikitsāsthāna 1.4); 1 part garlic juice to 8 parts milk, reduced; taken as a cold-season rasāyana. The classical *lasooney ka doodh* / "garlic milk" survives in modern North-Indian folk medicine.
- *Laśuna-tail / Lasun-tel* — garlic-medicated oil; for topical use on chronic joint disorders, paralysis, sciatic pain; classical pain-and-paralysis liniment.
- *Laśunādi vaṭi* — garlic-based compound pill; for vātavyādhi and chronic respiratory disease.
- *Lasun chutney / Lasun rasam* — South Indian culinary-medical preparations using garlic as a dietary-medicinal flavoring.
- *Garlic in trikatu and compound digestive powders* — by extension on the warming-pungent axis.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

A particularly rich Greco-Roman and continuous European tradition.

- *Egyptian (pre-Greco-Roman)*: Herodotus and Pliny record that *garlic and onions were a foundational ration of the pyramid-building workers*. The Ebers Papyrus (~1550 BCE) lists garlic in numerous medicinal recipes. The substance carries the Egyptian *strengthening-and-protective* register.
- *Greco-Roman*:
  - *Hippocratic Corpus*: extensive use; for gynaecological disorders, asthma, pulmonary, gastrointestinal.
  - *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ II.181-182): treats garlic at length — purges the bowels, relieves throat, anthelminthic, antitoxin, snakebite, beneficial against bad water in foreign travel.
  - *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XX.50-57): documents *61 medicinal disorders treated with garlic* — the most extensive single-drug treatment in Pliny.
  - *Galen*: extensive treatment; calls garlic "*country folk's theriac*" — the direct Greek/Latin source of the Arabic _Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ_ appellation.
  - *Virgil*: the famous *Moretum* (a poem attributed to Virgil's youth) describes a peasant's preparation of *moretum* — a paste of garlic, herbs, cheese, oil, and vinegar — as the foundational rustic Mediterranean food.
  - *Roman soldiery and gladiators*: garlic in the standard ration; the Roman military-medical tradition.
- *Medieval European*:
  - The *Bald's Leechbook* (Anglo-Saxon 10th c.) records numerous garlic preparations including the famous *garlic-onion-wine-bile MRSA-eye-salve* documented in 2015 by Nottingham researchers as having genuine antibiotic activity against modern MRSA.
  - Hildegard von Bingen treats garlic with characteristic ambivalence — strong, useful, but to be eaten cooked rather than raw for digestive ease.
- *Renaissance and early modern*:
  - The *plague-prophylactic register*: medieval and early-modern European folk medicine used garlic-and-vinegar preparations (the famous *Four Thieves Vinegar*, attributed legendarily to grave-robbers in plague-era Marseille who claimed garlic-and-vinegar protected them from contagion).
  - Garlic at the threshold against vampires and evil spirits — the European folk-protective register paralleling the Sanskrit *bhūtaghna* attribution; the substance carries cross-cultural apotropaic significance.
- *Modern Western pharmacy*:
  - Galenic preparations: _Oleum Allii_, _Tinctura Allii_, _Syrupus Allii_.
  - Modern garlic supplements: standardized for *allicin* content or *aged-garlic-extract* (AGE) — Kyolic and similar products.
  - Modern phytopharmacy: garlic is one of the most-studied medicinal plants of the late 20th and 21st centuries.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

See above. The principal preparations are *Sufūf-i Thūm*, *Marham-i Thūm*, *Toum / Sikanjabīn-i Thūm*, and *Joshānda-yi Thūm*.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Garlic is a *pan-climatic* substance — cultivated from the Mediterranean to South Asia to East Asia to the New World, in climates ranging from warm-dry Mediterranean to cool-damp temperate to subtropical-humid. The classical cultivation centers are *Mediterranean* (cradle of the canonical Greco-Roman use), *Central Asian / Persian* (the historical Western Regions / 西域 source for the Han-period Chinese introduction), *South Asian* (substantial cultivation in India and Pakistan), and *East Asian*. See _climates.typ_ for the framework.

*Warm-dry Mediterranean (the canonical cultivation and traditional-use region).* Hot dry summers and cool wet winters favor the bulb-and-clove storage strategy. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms the climate carries strong _grīṣma_ (summer-heat) Pitta load with mild winter Vāta phases; in 中醫 terms the climate is 暑燥 (summer-heat-dryness) on Lung-and-Heart with mild winter 寒 on Kidney; in Tibb terms the constitutional load is Hot-Dry (ṣafrā' / bilious) in summer and Cold-Dry (sawdā' / melancholic) in winter. *The classical Mediterranean use* of garlic in this climate is concentrated in the *cool-to-cold season* — winter and shoulder-seasons — when the warming-pungent character is constitutionally appropriate; the *summer use* is moderate and typically combined with cooling muṣliḥ (lemon, mint, vinegar in the *toum / aioli* register). The Mediterranean *moretum / aïoli / toum / skordalia* tradition — garlic paste with oil and other ingredients — observes the muṣliḥ rule constitutionally.

*Central Asian / Persian (warm-dry continental).* The Persian-Central-Asian region is the original cultivation center for the cultivated _A. sativum_ and the historical source of the Chinese introduction. The climate carries extreme seasonal range — hot dry summers, cold winters — and the *Persian-Tajik-Uzbek culinary use of garlic* in laghman, plov, and other rice-and-meat dishes is climate-appropriate to the cold-winter Vāta-equivalent register.

*South Asian (hot-humid monsoonal).* The Indian climate carries the strongest annual Pitta-Kapha load; classical Ayurvedic *seasonal restriction* of garlic to winter and convalescent use reflects the constitutional logic. The *South Indian Kerala-Karnataka-Tamil rasam-and-pulisseri* register uses garlic as a cooking-pot warming-aromatic but rarely as a heavy seasoning. The *North Indian Punjabi-Mughlai* register uses garlic substantially throughout the cool-season cooking. The *vegetarian-religious* exclusion (orthodox brahminical, temple-priestly, Jain, certain Vaiṣṇava lineages) is the religious-dietary overlay distinct from the constitutional-medical use.

*Subtropical-humid East Asian.* Garlic entered China from the Western Regions in the Han period and was rapidly integrated into Chinese cuisine; the warming-pungent character is constitutionally appropriate to the damp-and-cold winter half of the southern Chinese climate. Northeast Asian (Korean) cuisine uses garlic extraordinarily heavily — the *kimchi* tradition, the *bulgogi* marinades, the daily-consumption pattern — far exceeding the Chinese and Japanese use; the kimchi fermentation modifies the allicin chemistry substantively, transforming the substance into a microbiome-supportive fermented food.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Garlic exported globally as both fresh-bulb spice and functional-food / nutraceutical supplement reaches every climate. The constitutional logic still applies — heavy raw-garlic intake in hot-humid tropics in summer-Pitta seasons is constitutionally inappropriate (this is the classical Ayurvedic _kāla-viruddha_); the same intake in cool-damp Atlantic European winters is constitutionally apt. *Modern aged-garlic extract (AGE) supplements transcend the climate question* by virtue of the dramatically reduced pungent-irritant fraction; the Kyolic and similar products provide the substantive cardiovascular and immunomodulatory pharmacology with substantially reduced GI irritation, making the substance more climate-neutral.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Composition, garlic, raw, approximate per 100 g* — verify against USDA FoodData Central.

- Energy ~150 kcal; Water ~59 g
- Protein ~6.4 g; Fat ~0.5 g; Carbohydrate ~33 g; Dietary fiber ~2.1 g
- Manganese ~1.7 mg (substantial); Selenium ~14 µg
- Vitamin C ~31 mg; Vitamin B6 ~1.2 mg

*Key constituents (the pharmacologically distinctive fraction):*
- *Alliin (S-allyl cysteine sulfoxide)* — the *precursor compound* present in intact garlic cells (~1% of fresh garlic by weight).
- *Alliinase* — the enzyme present in adjacent cells; *when garlic is crushed, alliinase contacts alliin and converts it within seconds to allicin*.
- *Allicin (diallyl thiosulfinate)* — the *principal active*, formed by the alliin + alliinase reaction; *unstable* — decomposes within hours at room temperature, faster at high heat; the *characteristic raw-garlic pungency and tear-inducing volatile is allicin*. *The allicin is essentially destroyed by cooking* — which is why classical pharmacy distinguished *raw* (strongly antimicrobial, irritating) from *cooked* (mild, digestive, suitable for prolonged use).
- *Diallyl sulfide, diallyl disulfide, diallyl trisulfide, ajoene* — the *stable decomposition products* of allicin; the cooked-garlic and aged-garlic pharmacology rests on these compounds.
- *S-allyl cysteine (SAC)* — the *principal active in aged-black-garlic*; water-soluble, stable, well-characterized cardiovascular pharmacology; the modern *aged garlic extract (AGE)* products (Kyolic, etc.) are standardized for SAC content.
- *Saponins, flavonoids, fructans*.

*The allicin-and-derivatives chemistry — biochemically vindicated:*
- The classical Ayurvedic *guru-snigdha-tīkṣṇa* guṇa profile is a clean dravyaguna reading of the volatile-sulfur-and-fixed-oil fraction.
- The classical *raw-vs-cooked saṃskāra distinction* (Ayurveda's preference for the *kṣīrapāka* milk-cooking; Chinese pharmacy's distinction of raw vs. fried; Roman cooking with abundant garlic vs. raw chewed garlic as soldier's prophylactic) maps directly onto the allicin-vs-stable-derivatives chemistry. *Raw garlic = high allicin, strong antimicrobial, irritating; cooked garlic = stable sulfides, milder, suitable for prolonged use.* The classical-modern alignment is exact.
- The *aged-garlic extract* (AGE) — the modern Korean-Japanese-Western standardized form — chemically parallels the classical _kṣīrapāka_ preparation in transforming the harsh-pungent raw substance into a stable, well-tolerated, cardiovascular-supportive form. The pharmacological alignment is one of the cleanest cross-system convergences.

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antimicrobial (broad-spectrum)*: allicin has documented activity against gram-positive bacteria, gram-negative bacteria, mycobacteria, fungi, and certain protozoa and helminths; the *historical "country folk's theriac" / Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ* reading is mechanistically vindicated. Activity against *MRSA, Helicobacter pylori, Candida albicans* is well-characterized. The 2015 Nottingham reconstruction of *Bald's eyesalve* (10th c. Anglo-Saxon) demonstrated genuine antibiotic activity against modern MRSA — a striking classical-modern alignment.
- *Cardiovascular*: modest blood-pressure lowering effect (meta-analyses show ~5-10 mmHg systolic reduction with regular consumption); modest lipid effects (slight reduction in total and LDL cholesterol); *modest antiplatelet activity* (the antiplatelet effect is real and clinically relevant for the anticoagulant interaction caveat); modest endothelial-function improvements.
- *Anti-cancer (epidemiological + preclinical)*: substantial epidemiological signal for reduced *gastric, colorectal*, and possibly other cancers with regular garlic consumption; substantial preclinical evidence for the diallyl trisulfide and ajoene fractions; clinical translation is preliminary but the epidemiological signal is consistent.
- *Immunomodulatory*: NK cell activity enhancement; modest evidence for cold-and-flu prophylaxis in regular consumers.
- *Antiparasitic*: documented activity against *Giardia*, intestinal helminths; the cross-system Ayurvedic-Chinese-European _krimi-ghna_ / 殺蟲 / anthelminthic register.
- *Hypoglycemic*: modest; clinically relevant in concert with antidiabetic medications.
- *Hepatoprotective*: preclinical.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: preclinical broad-spectrum.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the classical Ayurvedic *Vāta-vyādhi-rasāyana* + Chinese *清熱解毒 / 殺蟲 / 健脾* + Tibb *Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ / muqāwim li-l-sumūm / qātil li-l-dūd* + Greco-Roman *country-folk theriac, pyramid-builder ration, 61-disorder Pliny* + modern allicin antimicrobial chemistry — *garlic is one of the cleanest four-system-convergence drugs in materia medica*, with the classical antibiotic-cardiovascular-anthelminthic-tonic register essentially vindicated by modern pharmacology.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary use*: up to ~4 cloves (~12 g) per day is the typical dietary register; well-tolerated cooked.
- *Therapeutic raw / supplement use*: 1-3 fresh cloves per day (~3-9 g) raw, or equivalent standardized supplement (typically 600-1200 mg AGE per day); higher doses for short-term therapeutic use.
- *Classical Ayurvedic kṣīrapāka rasāyana*: per Caraka, with qualified Ayurvedic supervision.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously)*:
  - *Pre-surgical*: discontinue high-dose garlic 7-10 days before surgery (antiplatelet caveat).
  - *Concurrent anticoagulants*: warfarin, NOAC, aspirin, clopidogrel — additive bleeding risk.
  - *Concurrent HIV protease inhibitors* (especially saquinavir) — *avoid*.
  - *Concurrent antidiabetic drugs* — monitor.
  - *Pregnancy*: dietary use accepted; high-dose supplements not recommended.
  - *Severe GI inflammation*: raw garlic substantially irritating.
  - *Pitta-pattern / bilious patients* (classical caveat preserved).
  - *Pre-mosque attendance* (the Tibb al-Nabawī etiquette — religious-cultural, not medical).
- *Sourcing*: organic, fresh, ideally local-and-seasonal; the *Chinese export garlic* market has been documented to have pesticide and bleaching-chemical concerns; reputable sourcing matters.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

